# LTL temporal operators: syntax, parsing pitfalls, and normalization.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# Section 5 of the demos covers the LTL features: the temporal operators
# (this demo), satisfiability and realizability of temporal formulas
# (demo_5.2), executing them (demo_5.3), and temporal specifications over
# combinations of Boolean algebras (demo_5.4).

# A specification talks about streams indexed by time, and the name prefix
# decides who controls a stream: outputs o1, o2, ... are chosen by the
# system, inputs i1, i2, ... by the environment. Nothing else marks them --
# the prefix is the whole convention.

# The always operator holds at every time point. Three spellings parse to
# the same formula -- G, always, and [] -- and all print back as always:

G (o1[t] = 1)
always o1[t] = 1
[] o1[t] = 1

# Note that the history position did not advance: all three spellings
# produced the same formula, which was already stored.

# sometimes asserts that a formula holds at some time point. It too has
# three spellings -- sometimes, <>, and the LTL eventually F -- and they
# are all the same operator, printing back as sometimes:

sometimes o2[t] = 1
<> o2[t] = 1
F (o2[t] = 1)

# The binary temporal operators are infix: U (until), R (release), W (weak
# until), and the past operators S (since) and T (trigger). Each also has
# a word spelling -- until, release, weak_until, since and trigger -- and,
# as with the always and sometimes spellings above, both spellings of an
# operator parse to the very same formula, so the word form re-entered
# right after its letter form does not advance the history position:

(o1[t] = 1) U (i1[t] = 1)
(o1[t] = 1) until (i1[t] = 1)
(o1[t] = 1) R (i1[t] = 1)
(o1[t] = 1) release (i1[t] = 1)
(o1[t] = 1) W (i1[t] = 1)
(o1[t] = 1) weak_until (i1[t] = 1)
(o1[t] = 1) S (i1[t] = 1)
(o1[t] = 1) since (i1[t] = 1)
(o1[t] = 1) T (i1[t] = 1)
(o1[t] = 1) trigger (i1[t] = 1)

# The infix operators bind tighter than && and ||, so a compound operand
# wants its own parentheses, just as with the prefix operators below.

# There is no next operator. Adjacent time points are reached with offsets
# instead, as in o1[t] = i1[t-1], which demo_5.3 executes.

# A parsing pitfall: a prefix temporal operator takes the whole rest of the
# formula as its operand, so the conjunction below ends up UNDER the G:

G (o1[t] = 1) && (o2[t] = 1)

# To combine two temporal formulas, parenthesize each one:

(G (o1[t] = 1)) && (F (o2[t] = 1))

# The whatis command reports what kind of node an expression is:

whatis F o1[t] = 1
whatis o1[t]
whatis {181}:bv[8]

# normalize keeps the temporal operators in place and normalizes the
# non-temporal formulas under them. Under this F -- which prints back in
# its canonical spelling, sometimes -- quantifier elimination discards the
# trivially satisfiable conjunct:

n F ((o1[t] = 1) && (ex y (o2[t] = y)))

# Under U and T, a duplicated conjunct is dropped:

n (o1[t] = 1 && o1[t] = 1) U (o2[t] = 1)
n ((o1[t] = 1 && o1[t] = 1)) T (o2[t] = 1)

# Under R, the tautologous right-hand side collapses to T:

n (o1[t] = 0) R (o2[t] o2[t]' = 0)

# Under W and S, the non-temporal quantifiers are eliminated outright:

n (all x (o1[t] x = 0)) W (o2[t] = 1)
n (ex y (o1[t] = y && o2[t] = 1)) S (o3[t] = 1)

# The canonical form writes a term equal to 1 as its complement equal to 0:

n always o1[t] = 1 && o1[t-1] = 1

# And an inner contradiction sinks the whole formula:

n G (o1[t] != o1[t])

# qelim likewise reaches under a temporal operator to eliminate the
# non-temporal quantifiers:

qelim G (all x (o1[t] x = 0))

# Beyond LTL there is a CTL* fragment with the path quantifiers A (on all
# paths) and E (on some path). The deciding commands reject them until the
# fragment is switched:

sat E F (o1[t] = 1)

fragment ctl_star

E F (o1[t] = 1)
sat E F (o1[t] = 1)
sat A G (o1[t] = 1)

# fragment ltl switches back, which is also the default:

fragment ltl

quit
